==English==

===Etymology===
{{unk|en}}.

In sense “destroyed”, perhaps corruption of {{cog|fr|fougasse||land mine}} ({{w|Flame fougasse|flame fougasses}} were used extensively in the Korean and Vietnam wars); also corrupted as {{m|en|foo gas}}. Alternatively, acronym of “fucked up, got ambushed, zipped in [a body bag]” – compare {{m|en|fubar}} – but this may be a {{glossary|backronym}}

In sense “fake”, popularized by film ''[[w:Donnie Brasco (film)|Donnie Brasco]]'' (1997), based on book ''[[w:Donnie Brasco: My Undercover Life in the Mafia|Donnie Brasco: My Undercover Life in the Mafia]]'' (1988), presumably of New York area Mafia origin, from the Fugazy Continental limousine company in New York (owned by William “Bill” Fugazy), due to cheesy “look like a rich guy” ads of the 1970s and 1980s, or due to alleged poor business practices by the company.<ref name="wd">{{cite web |url=http://www.word-detective.com/2008/02/fugazy-redux/ |title=Fugazy redux: Looking for the real fake. |work=The Word Detective |date=2008-02-12}}</ref> Possibly influenced by {{cog|it||fu cazzo|it was shit}}.

Less plausible derivations are from English {{m|en|fugacious||fleeting}} or cognates such as {{cog|it|fugace}}.

